Z||000000||burdell1||09-03-2000||01:47 PM||||Well, I have parked my Fiero for the past couple months because it finally died (they said that it is probably a couple of bad pistons). I got a new car (a Sunfire) but I do want to get my Fiero fixed (no way would I trade my baby in.) Any ideas of where to take it on such a limited budget? I thought about bringing it to a local vo-tech and see what they could do. Has anyone tried that before?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||02:27 PM||||If the problem is 100% mechanical, I'd probably do it.  You won't get any performance mods done, but it should be back to stock.  Only problem is time.  My local votech only works on a few cars a year and usually they pick one older, carbbed car and a couple of much newer, FIed, computerized cars.  Since the Fiero falls in between, they might not see the practicality of it.  Wouldn't hurt to try, though.  The fact that you live in Minneapolis (much larger than my town of 13,000) might be a big advantage as they should be larger and maybe work on more cars during the year.  Be prepared to give up your car for most, if not all, of the school year.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||09-04-2000||05:47 AM||||If you want to wait a few months, I'm hopefully gonna be enrolled at Hennipen Tech for automotive engine rebuilding and machining.  If you need any help feel free to e-mail me.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
